 37. STUDENT: Human.\nTurn 38. TUTOR: Human. Got it.\nTurn 39. TUTOR: Oh, that sounds like fun.\nTurn 40. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 6 seconds]\nTurn 40. TUTOR: I'm gonna, you wanna dive right in to math or do you wanna play like a game first?\nTurn 41. STUDENT: Dive in.\nTurn 42. TUTOR: Diving in. Okay. Let me, let's do our,\nTurn 43. TUTOR: do we have a bonus question?\nTurn 44. STUDENT: Maybe.\nTurn 45. TUTOR: Question of the day. Question of the day. Let's do this. Warm up.\nTurn 46. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 2 seconds]\nTurn 46. TUTOR: Okay. This is like pattern recognition. If you know that six times seven is forty-two, which of the following is true?\nTurn 47. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: p1 - 'If you know that 7 x 6 = 42, which of the following is true?' (start)] The screen shows a multiple-choice question. \"If you know that 7 x 6 = 42, which of the following is true?\" The options are: A) 70 x 60 = 42 x 10, B) 70 x 6 = 4200, C) 70 x 60 = 4200, D) 70 x 60 = 420.\nTurn 47. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 5 seconds] Student selects option C.\nTurn 47. STUDENT: Seven times sixty equals forty-two, uh, not minus. Seventy, wait. Seventy times sixty equals forty-two times ten. Then seventy times six equals four thousand two hundred. Seventy times sixty equals forty-two thousand. Seventy times sixty equals four hundred and twenty. Um,\n\nRespond as the TUTOR. Give only your response, no labels or prefixes.\n\nNEXT REPLY INSTRUCTION:\nPush for appropriate rigor now. Build on what the learner has actually demonstrated, not an answer the tutor supplied. Invite the student to justify a step, connect representations, check a claim, generalize, or take the next step independently. Choose one opportunity that fits the current problem. Do not give the reasoning before asking for it, turn the challenge into a sequence of tiny leading hints, or introduce unrelated difficulty. Keep the mathematical work with the learner; rigor is not simply saying why after every answer.\nFor this next reply only, use the specified poli